call_details.response:
The current position has two pieces for each player, with Black (o) occupying (row 4, column 3) and (row 5, column 4). These two stones are adjacent to the empty square (row 4, column 4). Occupying that square simultaneously connects Black’s stones (creating multiple open two-in-a-rows horizontally and vertically) and blocks the same square from White.  

A static enumeration of every winning four-in-a-row line shows that the move \(\text{x}\to(4,4)\) yields the highest immediate score of any legal placement: it upgrades an existing diagonal into a two-in-a-row, claims four new lines, and converts three of Black’s one-in-a-rows into mixed (dead) lines.  

After this placement the only replies that restore a competitive score for Black are those that themselves create two-in-a-rows (principally \(\text{o}\to(3,3)\)). In every such branch White can immediately answer by creating a three-in-a-row threat on the bottom rank (\(\text{x}\to\) column 1). Black is forced to block on the edge, after which the resulting position evaluates to approximately \(-19\) (White to move). All alternative first moves leave Black a way to reach a strictly lower score:

- column 3 lets Black connect at (4,4) at once and finishes at approximately \(-35\);
- column 2 gifts Black an immediate diagonal three-in-a-row and finishes below \(-90\);
- the pure forcing moves (columns 0 or 1) merely transpose, after best play, into the same \(-19\) position already obtained by playing column 4 first;
- wing moves (columns 5/6) again allow the powerful connection at (4,4) and finish even lower.

Because column 4 is the unique move that both maximises White’s own line-count and prevents Black’s most valuable connection, while every deviation permits Black a demonstrably superior counter-play, it is the optimal choice.

Final Answer: 4

